WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.816
Decision 3: the tool table and the trifecta.

2
00:00:03.096 --> 00:00:13.016
Fill in the table from lesson 10 for every tool the agent can call: read or write, reversible or not, credential and scope, and what the worst call could do.

3
00:00:13.296 --> 00:00:16.645
Then do lesson 11’s trifecta analysis out loud.

4
00:00:16.925 --> 00:00:27.208
For the default project: issue bodies and comments are untrusted content (anyone who can file an issue writes them, and on a public repository that is anyone).

5
00:00:27.488 --> 00:00:34.805
Private data may be absent if you use a public repository and nothing else, which is a legitimate way to remove a leg.

6
00:00:35.085 --> 00:00:40.589
But many real versions add something private: internal notes, customer names, a roadmap.

7
00:00:40.869 --> 00:00:42.363
If yours does, say so.

8
00:00:42.643 --> 00:00:46.504
The outbound leg is the post, plus anything that fetches URLs.

9
00:00:46.784 --> 00:00:50.368
Then pick your structural defense and say which leg it addresses.

10
00:00:50.648 --> 00:01:01.997
A map-reduce reader that turns each issue into a fixed-schema record with no URLs and no free text longer than a sentence makes injected instructions much harder to carry forward.

11
00:01:02.277 --> 00:01:06.309
A fixed action set means nothing in an issue can add a destination.

12
00:01:06.589 --> 00:01:17.213
A deterministic guard (allowlisted channel, no links to unlisted hosts, no private values in outbound text) is the control that holds when the model is fooled.

13
00:01:17.493 --> 00:01:21.376
You built that guard in lab 04; adapting it is fair game.
